WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:09.579
John Talburt: All right, well, thanks for joining. We spent a lot of time last Tuesday talking about

2
00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:16.200
John Talburt: the course itself, and about the information quality program. I thought that was important.

3
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:22.619
John Talburt: So today, we're actually going to start on the content and really talk a little bit about information quality.

4
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:28.570
John Talburt: And we… Started this course, like, 20 years ago.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:37.400
John Talburt: And, we thought about, and we needed some course that was a foundations course, and so we decided to call it Principles.

6
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:41.249
John Talburt: And then, I quickly learned that

7
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:48.160
John Talburt: There weren't any really real established principles, so we decided we would design some.

8
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:56.489
John Talburt: So today, I want to go over what I think are some 7 underlying principles of the information quality discipline.

9
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.150
John Talburt: So, let me get…

10
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:08.360
John Talburt: Slides going. And some of this we talked a little bit about when we talked about the program. We talked about this, what is information quality?

11
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:17.960
John Talburt: And again, there's a lot of different ways people describe it. Again, the most common is if you ask somebody what it is just off the street.

12
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:22.890
John Talburt: If they have any answer at all, it'll probably be accuracy that the data are accurate.

13
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.820
John Talburt: But that's a little bit problematic. We'll talk more about that.

14
00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:35.230
John Talburt: Then, you know, there are other attributes, and we'll talk about this as well. So, how do we describe the quality of data?

15
00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:41.050
John Talburt: And that leads us to something we'll talk about in the history of this topic.

16
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:50.129
John Talburt: Is this idea of… dimensions or characteristics of data. So, a couple of these are completeness and timeliness.

17
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:55.889
John Talburt: The idea that it's free of defects… This is a good one.

18
00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:07.440
John Talburt: This one we talked about before is really quite accurate in that the way this entire topic evolved is that we borrowed

19
00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:13.100
John Talburt: From total quality management, from manufacturing, and started deploying it to information systems.

20
00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:19.509
John Talburt: and others. So there's many ways that people describe. Probably one of the most

21
00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:23.010
John Talburt: Commonly one… common ones that you hear is.

22
00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.380
John Talburt: Fitness for use, or fitness for purpose.

23
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:36.649
John Talburt: And it's a good one. It actually comes from Joseph Duran, who was one of the gurus of the total quality management in manufacturing.

24
00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:43.099
John Talburt: And, but when… but again, this is kind of where we reach, dichotomy.

25
00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:48.970
John Talburt: When he said that, when Duran said that, he was talking about the finished product.

26
00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:58.340
John Talburt: He says, is this product what you really need? Does it… does it fit your needs? Is it… is it fit for use?

27
00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:08.230
John Talburt: if they're building a car, a toaster, whatever, is this thing that we built. So, even there, we get the idea that we're talking about a product.

28
00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:26.849
John Talburt: And so, again, a lot of people, when they use that and they say, well, data quality is fit for use, for fitness, data that's fit for use, they're talking about the… the source data. They're talking about the data coming in the door.

29
00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:41.979
John Talburt: And you can… you can say that about that data, but that data has not been made into a product or a service yet. And so, it begs the question, what do I need to do with this raw data that's coming in?

30
00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:48.570
John Talburt: What quality does it need in order, when I put it all together into a product.

31
00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:51.390
John Talburt: That the product has quality.

32
00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:57.100
John Talburt: So, it's a little bit of a debate, and we can talk about that as we go along.

33
00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:08.470
John Talburt: But I… so, anyway, what we decided to do is just come up with our own definition of it, and we talked about this earlier. So, there's two main parts.

34
00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:19.270
John Talburt: Maximizing the value and minimizing the risk of an organization's information assets. So this is sort of an internal view. I'm in an organization, or I own an organization.

35
00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:23.029
John Talburt: Or maybe it's just me, and I have…

36
00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:31.250
John Talburt: data, and so part of this is how do I make the most use, or get the most value out of that data?

37
00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:38.539
John Talburt: But then looking at it from the external side is, okay, I'm producing some kind of product or service.

38
00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:50.370
John Talburt: Then it should create value or be valuable to the… to the people or systems that consume that data, whatever, whether it's a person or a system consuming that data.

39
00:04:50.600 --> 00:05:05.190
John Talburt: So, there's, again, there's always this dichotomy. There's two sides to the coin, as they say. So, information quality is about how it creates value for the organization that has that information or data.

40
00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:18.370
John Talburt: And also, is it producing, in sort of the manufacturing sense, an information product or service that's creating value for the consumers, for the users, valuable to them?

41
00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:21.300
John Talburt: Or, as Duran would say, it's fit for use.

42
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.529
John Talburt: It's very interesting, I just was on the phone today with another,

43
00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:31.329
John Talburt: A very notable person in the area of data quality.

44
00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:35.129
John Talburt: that you may have heard of before is Danette McGilvery.

45
00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:37.760
John Talburt: The net has been, in the…

46
00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:44.919
John Talburt: the data quality, I've been a consultant practitioner for many, many years, and has written a couple of books, and…

47
00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:53.129
John Talburt: And she has developed what's called a 10-step method for executing data quality projects. She's very knowledgeable.

48
00:05:53.740 --> 00:06:03.910
John Talburt: we were just, talking about this today. It's interesting, though, that in her definition of information quality.

49
00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:11.979
John Talburt: Her emphasis is on the trust. So, as a matter of fact, that's the name of her book, is…

50
00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:18.210
John Talburt: Of executing data quality projects to produce trusted data.

51
00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:26.470
John Talburt: So, however you want to look at it is the main thing in our definition is value, and hers is trust.

52
00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:31.059
John Talburt: And I think they're quite similar to each other. Now.

53
00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:33.390
John Talburt: When we talk about value, though.

54
00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:37.710
John Talburt: I also want to be a little more explicit about that.

55
00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:39.000
John Talburt: And,

56
00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:49.300
John Talburt: the way I see it, there are 3 primary value propositions. When we talk about maximizing value for an organization, we immediately think of

57
00:06:49.550 --> 00:07:00.259
John Talburt: A business, and the value, of course, of being an asset would be something we measure in dollars, or currency, depending on what country we're in.

58
00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:08.189
John Talburt: And that definitely is one. Value could be… the value proposition could be making a profit.

59
00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:21.359
John Talburt: But there are a lot of other organizations that are engaged in other ways, and a couple of other ways, I think, the two other main categories are… one is quality of life.

60
00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:28.629
John Talburt: That the value proposition, they're trying to improve the quality of life, and of course, that immediately brings us to…

61
00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:33.190
John Talburt: Medically related, hospitals, things like that, physicians.

62
00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:42.880
John Talburt: And so, even though, obviously, they do make money, and they need money to go on, their primary value that they're trying to bring is they're trying to bring

63
00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:48.910
John Talburt: Better quality of life by Curing diseases and, and,

64
00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:55.479
John Talburt: Developing, medicines and things that make life better for people.

65
00:07:55.990 --> 00:08:00.739
John Talburt: And yet another, are the non-profits.

66
00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:14.549
John Talburt: that have, as their value proposition, they have missions to accomplish. So, value for them is accomplishing their mission. And this is a case where dollars don't seem to count.

67
00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:19.709
John Talburt: Because, for example, the military is willing to spend whatever money it needs

68
00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:25.790
John Talburt: To accomplish particular missions that it's on. We're right in the middle of that right now.

69
00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:42.620
John Talburt: So… so not all… when we talk about value, and creating value from… or maximizing value from information, it's not just helping people make money, it also, can help improve the quality of life.

70
00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:45.710
John Talburt: Help organizations accomplish their missions.

71
00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:52.029
John Talburt: I think this covers most of the value propositions, but if you can think of another one, let me know.

72
00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:59.779
John Talburt: So, a little bit of history, of information quality.

73
00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:11.419
John Talburt: Is the… there's some people, I think, Jan Lee, who… who was the first author in this book that you have called Journey to Data Quality.

74
00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:17.440
John Talburt: I heard a lecture by her one time, and she went all the way back to…

75
00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:27.359
John Talburt: To historical times in the, and the, this very famous,

76
00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:32.359
John Talburt: Rosetta Stone that Napoleon's troops found in Egypt.

77
00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:36.270
John Talburt: Talked about how that solves some data quality problems, so…

78
00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.750
John Talburt: But I won't go that far back.

79
00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:45.130
John Talburt: To me, the problem as we know it, the problem of recognition of poor information quality.

80
00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:54.450
John Talburt: I call that the data cleansing phase, and that happened when,

81
00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:00.480
John Talburt: People started trying to build data warehouses, and this is back in the 80s.

82
00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:06.160
John Talburt: So, all of you are probably so young, you think there's always been data warehouses, but actually.

83
00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:12.330
John Talburt: Back in the early days of computing, there wasn't a concept of a data warehouse.

84
00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:27.349
John Talburt: And, when that came along, everybody really embraced that idea, yeah, we should take all of our data from all of our different departments, put it together for, like, a year, and analyze it, and see if we can do better in our business.

85
00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:33.900
John Talburt: That'll give us tremendous business information, or business intelligence, as they call it, BI.

86
00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:49.160
John Talburt: And so that was a really new idea, and everybody immediately said, yeah, that's great. So everybody started doing that, and what they really discovered when they tried to do that was that because all of these departments in the business

87
00:10:49.230 --> 00:11:07.639
John Talburt: were doing their own computing, they often had their own computer systems, they had their own data architects and people designing things, and, you know, things were not quite as well organized in the early days. When they tried to put the data together, it was almost impossible, and they had a huge failure rate.

88
00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:10.280
John Talburt: And projects to build data warehouses.

89
00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:18.649
John Talburt: And, out of that, Emerged a business, Information International.

90
00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:28.790
John Talburt: That, started, had huge success by going around and,

91
00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:33.160
John Talburt: Helping companies straighten out their data warehouses.

92
00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:42.759
John Talburt: And so this, these, really, information quality started not in academia, but it really started in business.

93
00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:49.529
John Talburt: By consultants, primarily, and… which turned out to be a little bit of a problem to, in the beginning.

94
00:11:49.750 --> 00:12:00.180
John Talburt: Because, consultants always want to distinguish themselves, so they kept using different terminology about describing, data and information quality problems.

95
00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:07.179
John Talburt: But, the guy who started, Information Impact International, a guy named Larry English.

96
00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:14.479
John Talburt: Really, he wrote a couple of books about data quality in warehouses, or information quality in warehouses.

97
00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:17.700
John Talburt: And, made a great contribution.

98
00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:23.779
John Talburt: Unfortunately, he passed away a couple of years ago. He's a great guy, I got to know him very well.

99
00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:31.050
John Talburt: But anyway, this is the beginning. The real beginning, from an information system standpoint, if you want to…

100
00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:33.779
John Talburt: not worry about the Rosetta Stone.

101
00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:40.450
John Talburt: Is, happened in the data warehousing movement, back in the 1980s.

102
00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:43.110
John Talburt: And so, here is…

103
00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:58.099
John Talburt: Where they began to recognize that you really needed to pay attention to the quality of data. And then the main problem they were having, of course, with data warehousing is a problem that still persists that we call inconsistent representation.

104
00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:00.980
John Talburt: you know.

105
00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:16.029
John Talburt: one department was using AR for… to represent Arkansas, and another department was using ARK. So they were inconsistently representing the data. And so when they tried to put it all together into one.

106
00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:22.170
John Talburt: Data warehouse in order to examine it, and that's where they began to see all the problems.

107
00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:25.440
John Talburt: So,

108
00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:36.629
John Talburt: What was lucky for Larry, and the reason he made so much money in the early days, is that he just focused on this, they would have all these problems, and he would come in.

109
00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:43.280
John Talburt: And with his team, and with his knowledge, and he would straighten out the warehouse, right?

110
00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:47.699
John Talburt: And so, I guess to his own benefit.

111
00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:53.160
John Talburt: He… it took a while to get to the next phase, which was…

112
00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:58.030
John Talburt: To say, okay, well, we're having problems, what's the root cause of this?

113
00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:04.239
John Talburt: The root cause is that the sources of data that are coming out of these different departments and business units

114
00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:24.400
John Talburt: they're inconsistent in the way they're representing things, and they're not structuring things the same way. So, it's all this inconsistency, and so that was the root cause of most of the problems with the data warehouse. And so, in order to solve that, you have to add another phase called the prevention phase.

115
00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:35.689
John Talburt: And that's when people started realizing you needed to address the root causes of these problems, and not just try to fix the problems after they occur.

116
00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:40.069
John Talburt: And so this is the beginning, again, following along with

117
00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:45.399
John Talburt: Some of the main ideas of quality management that we find in manufacturing.

118
00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:51.620
John Talburt: people like Deming and Duran and Shughart and people like that, that were,

119
00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:54.259
John Talburt: Gurus, if you will, of quality.

120
00:14:54.570 --> 00:15:07.349
John Talburt: They had already discovered this, that you need to have a feedback loop, you need to learn from your mistakes and go back and try to fix the things that were causing them, so that you got continual improvement.

121
00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:19.739
John Talburt: So this is sort of the second wave of information quality, is where you needed to go back and do root cause analysis. Again, that's something that occurs in total quality management.

122
00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:22.710
John Talburt: And then,

123
00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:33.590
John Talburt: It took a little while, but the next phase, in the history was… and this was… now, even though most of all this was happening on the industry side, business side.

124
00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:48.790
John Talburt: this is kind of where academia steps in. So one of the greatest contributions from academia, which the institution most involved at this time was MIT in Cambridge, Massachusetts.

125
00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:54.230
John Talburt: And Dr. Rich Wong and his, A major professor there.

126
00:15:54.500 --> 00:16:00.189
John Talburt: we're very much into this idea of analogizing

127
00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:09.109
John Talburt: information quality management with total quality management and manufacturing. And again, in manufacturing.

128
00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:15.769
John Talburt: going back to Duran, the primary focus was on the end product. You start with the end in mind.

129
00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:24.169
John Talburt: So, this was then added to information quality to say, well, we need to manage information as a product.

130
00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:29.579
John Talburt: Not just look at the data sources and the individual files.

131
00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:38.199
John Talburt: If you just spend all your time cleaning that, it doesn't guarantee… you know, there's a, perhaps one of the most famous

132
00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:46.330
John Talburt: quotes is, GIGO, you're probably familiar with the GIGO, which is garbage in equals garbage out.

133
00:16:46.850 --> 00:17:01.259
John Talburt: And some people kind of misapplied the logic of that and said, well, if it's garbage in, you get garbage out. If you put clean in, or good in, you're gonna get good out. Of course, that doesn't logically follow.

134
00:17:01.530 --> 00:17:04.289
John Talburt: But some people kind of subscribe to that.

135
00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:21.849
John Talburt: And so, there's a certain school of thought that says, well, if I just clean up the sources and make them very consistent, make them accurate, make them timely, all the good things that we want, and I put them into my system, I'm guaranteed that I'll get a good information product.

136
00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:25.880
John Talburt: But, certainly, that's not true.

137
00:17:27.530 --> 00:17:35.070
John Talburt: even if you look in manufacturing, that's certainly not the way they look at it there, right? You could take,

138
00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:43.340
John Talburt: Building a car, You know, you could take and gold plate all of the…

139
00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:51.859
John Talburt: Axles and bearings and everything to… and make them much better, or make them out of titanium, or whatever.

140
00:17:51.960 --> 00:18:03.799
John Talburt: But that wouldn't guarantee that the car you're building is any better, and it still could be the wrong car. I'm a big fan of the El Camino, they don't make them anymore.

141
00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:07.949
John Talburt: Because people were not buying them. It's not what people really wanted.

142
00:18:08.230 --> 00:18:22.960
John Talburt: So, again, just because you have good, clean source data doesn't mean when you put it into your system and get an information product out, it'll be a good… it'll be a high-quality information product. So you really need to start with what

143
00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:26.649
John Talburt: I like a quote. There's another,

144
00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:30.090
John Talburt: Very famous person in this field, a guy named

145
00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:38.500
John Talburt: Tom Redman, Dr. Tom Redman, who started off his career at AT&T and got into data quality.

146
00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:42.430
John Talburt: He's called, the data doc, actually, he calls himself.

147
00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:44.179
John Talburt: And,

148
00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:55.639
John Talburt: he said, you have to really ask two questions. One question, is the data right? Which is, that is, is the data kind of correct?

149
00:18:55.770 --> 00:19:06.129
John Talburt: But is… the other question, is it the right data? And do I have the right data to build a product that I'm trying to produce? And we're going to look at an example of that here shortly.

150
00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:18.130
John Talburt: So again, in the third phase, kind of the history of information quality, is this realization that you really needed to start from the product side. You really need to manage information as product.

151
00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:34.490
John Talburt: The next realization, or the next phase, comes in when people realize that a lot of the problems that they were having, they're still having problems, even though they're taking this product perspective.

152
00:19:34.750 --> 00:19:40.639
John Talburt: They're cleaning data, they're trying to continuously improve their processes.

153
00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:54.870
John Talburt: that, sometimes the problem, the root cause, lies in the actual information architecture, the way you structured the data. And of course, most of this happened in the old days was around

154
00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:57.670
John Talburt: Relational databases.

155
00:19:57.810 --> 00:20:06.850
John Talburt: And of course, in a relational database, you always start with a model a schema, And,

156
00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:14.950
John Talburt: And invariably, that's… you start building or designing that schema before you put any data into the database.

157
00:20:15.070 --> 00:20:24.029
John Talburt: And so then you have to imagine, at the very beginning, all of the things that you want to do with the data, and try to accommodate that into your schema.

158
00:20:24.430 --> 00:20:38.870
John Talburt: Which is impossible to do. Things change. Everything changes, the sources of data change, the kinds of data, everything changes. And so, there's a famous quote, by,

159
00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:51.239
John Talburt: Oh, gosh, I've forgotten the guy's name. But anyway, he said, all models are wrong, but some are useful, and that's actually quite true.

160
00:20:51.310 --> 00:21:01.200
John Talburt: It's very useful to start off with some kind of model, but you're… but it's always going to be wrong. There's always going to be something you didn't anticipate, or you left out.

161
00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:05.970
John Talburt: No matter how many -oh columns that you put in your schema.

162
00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:10.339
John Talburt: So, this was a realization that to really have good

163
00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:27.610
John Talburt: quality information products and services, you needed a good information architecture that supported it. So that brings in this idea… and the dotted line here represents the difference between the implementation of an information system

164
00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:32.249
John Talburt: And it's designed in what we call an enterprise architecture.

165
00:21:32.730 --> 00:21:42.609
John Talburt: And of course, there are different views of what an enterprise architecture is, but… and what we're using is that it has four main components.

166
00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:47.290
John Talburt: The business architecture, what it is I'm trying to do in my business.

167
00:21:47.570 --> 00:22:03.300
John Talburt: the information architecture, what information do I need to accomplish that business? And once I know what the information is, how do I build a solution to use that information? And then, finally, what technical architecture do I need to support it?

168
00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:05.580
John Talburt: So, this is…

169
00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:13.479
John Talburt: Intended to be a way to look at how you start a business, or any kind of an enterprise.

170
00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:20.770
John Talburt: And so, here we're… we've moved away from just the implementation of the system in the lower part.

171
00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:27.849
John Talburt: To the first idea that we need to look at design, in this case, the design is the information architecture.

172
00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:31.299
John Talburt: And of course, then that tells us where we're going next.

173
00:22:31.510 --> 00:22:32.900
John Talburt: is that…

174
00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:42.469
John Talburt: Alright, if we really need to look at the information architecture, we really need to look at the whole business architecture, the whole enterprise architecture.

175
00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:51.659
John Talburt: Because now, in this phase of the history, is where we're beginning to look at information as an enterprise asset.

176
00:22:51.930 --> 00:22:56.749
John Talburt: And this brings a lot of other things into the picture besides just data quality.

177
00:22:57.040 --> 00:23:00.840
John Talburt: We're going to have to have data governance. If it's going to be an asset.

178
00:23:00.970 --> 00:23:04.910
John Talburt: We need to control it, we need to have some kind of program.

179
00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:11.149
John Talburt: That we call data governance, that gives us guidelines for how we make rules

180
00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:14.569
John Talburt: About, what we do with the data.

181
00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:18.499
John Talburt: And so, data governance comes into the picture.

182
00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:25.359
John Talburt: The idea of a chief data officer comes into the picture, someone who's thinking or looking about

183
00:23:25.480 --> 00:23:31.140
John Talburt: The, information and data across the entire enterprise.

184
00:23:31.570 --> 00:23:43.009
John Talburt: Data quality, of course, information quality, data integration, integrating data, master data management, and data analytics. So there's a lot of things involved when we take an enterprise view.

185
00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:48.380
John Talburt: So the fourth is, Enterprise Review.

186
00:23:50.890 --> 00:23:52.400
John Talburt: So what's next?

187
00:23:52.920 --> 00:24:01.299
John Talburt: What I'm seeing… is that… And we're… we'll talk a little bit about standards for this.

188
00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:05.350
John Talburt: Is the whole idea of interoperability.

189
00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:21.580
John Talburt: Everything that we've talked about in the first four phases of the development of information quality have been around one implementation, that is, one enterprise that's using it, and how it sort of evolved from discovering problems with the data warehouse.

190
00:24:21.750 --> 00:24:32.270
John Talburt: To continuous improvement, to managing information as product, to looking at the information architecture, to now looking at the entire

191
00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:42.159
John Talburt: enterprise architecture, and viewing information as an asset. So all this is around one enterprise, or around one implementation.

192
00:24:42.430 --> 00:24:48.659
John Talburt: So I think what's next, and what we're seeing is that, we need to collaborate.

193
00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:57.039
John Talburt: And so, some of the pioneers in this area are… In the medical field.

194
00:24:57.200 --> 00:25:06.180
John Talburt: Because, in medicine, we have, for a long time, had an entire ecosystem, right? So we have multiple

195
00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:10.180
John Talburt: Hospitals and clinics. We have multiple providers.

196
00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:17.590
John Talburt: Doctors, we support nurses, and then, of course, we have the payers, we have the insurance companies.

197
00:25:17.610 --> 00:25:35.289
John Talburt: And they're all sharing this information. It's not all just… we don't need it to be… well, we need it to be, but it's not necessarily just that it has to be consistent within one enterprise. We want it to somehow be consistent across multiple enterprises.

198
00:25:35.660 --> 00:25:40.650
John Talburt: And so… Again, healthcare's kind of been a primary of our…

199
00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:50.990
John Talburt: A leader in this, and because they've needed to share information about the same patients, and the same providers, and the same payers.

200
00:25:51.490 --> 00:25:59.839
John Talburt: And so, this idea of how do we share information, and we'll talk later about some standards around this.

201
00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:06.039
John Talburt: And another area that's very important, and that is in supply chains for enterprise, so…

202
00:26:06.190 --> 00:26:12.410
John Talburt: Especially large enterprises may have hundreds, maybe even thousands of suppliers.

203
00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:18.159
John Talburt: And so they need to be able to somehow exchange or interoperate with their data.

204
00:26:18.320 --> 00:26:24.150
John Talburt: So, this is where the next frontier is in data and information quality, I believe.

205
00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:28.399
John Talburt: Now, one that I haven't added a slide on this.

206
00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:32.510
John Talburt: Is that, but we will talk about this later.

207
00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:38.449
John Talburt: is what does AI have to do with it? And so I've got a whole lecture that we'll talk about.

208
00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:44.370
John Talburt: AI is not so much about what we're talking about here, about the interoperability of data.

209
00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:56.160
John Talburt: But it certainly is important, because the new large language models that are dominating are just data, really, so they are big data

210
00:26:57.770 --> 00:27:09.910
John Talburt: repositories, huge. We talk about billions, all getting up into trillions, I guess, of parameters. And a parameter is nothing but a conditional probability.

211
00:27:10.260 --> 00:27:13.310
John Talburt: It says, given this word or token.

212
00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:16.969
John Talburt: What's the probability that the next token will be this?

213
00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:25.990
John Talburt: If I have X, what's the probability it'll be followed by Y? So, that's a parameter, and so, obviously.

214
00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:39.349
John Talburt: There's many, many ways, many ways that one token could follow another, and that's the reason that there are so many of these parameters, so many billions of the parameters, so many ways that we can say things.

215
00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:44.459
John Talburt: So, but anyway, the point is that that is all data.

216
00:27:44.780 --> 00:27:50.489
John Talburt: And so, data quality is very important, but in a couple of ways. One is that,

217
00:27:53.160 --> 00:28:00.400
John Talburt: We need good data to build the models, and that's kind of where the focus is right now. But now there's a new focus starting.

218
00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:11.510
John Talburt: that we're working on, and that is the idea, if we have an AI model, can it help us improve data? So it's sort of a virtuous cycle.

219
00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:25.089
John Talburt: good data builds good models, and good models can help make good data. We'll talk more about that. But here, I think what we're talking about in the next phase of information quality is,

220
00:28:25.540 --> 00:28:27.550
John Talburt: Where we have shared standards.

221
00:28:27.890 --> 00:28:43.990
John Talburt: That help us with data interoperability and data portability, being able to move data from system to system and not get locked in where we start using a system and we want to change systems, but all of our data is in the

222
00:28:44.120 --> 00:28:53.699
John Talburt: the initial system, and we're not allowed, or there's not feasible, to take that data out and put it into another system. It's not portable for us.

223
00:28:55.340 --> 00:28:59.060
John Talburt: So there's a lot of emerging topics here, and

224
00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:06.660
John Talburt: So, there's the data quality standards, from ISO, we'll talk about those.

225
00:29:08.390 --> 00:29:17.979
John Talburt: There's data management for streaming. Most of the time, we've been talking about data quality management for large batches, large files of data, but streaming, or real-time data.

226
00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:24.810
John Talburt: Data quality for unstructured data, how do we… and again, this is evolving as well.

227
00:29:25.010 --> 00:29:27.890
John Talburt: Right now, people are saying that

228
00:29:28.070 --> 00:29:41.039
John Talburt: Well, I have a lot of data. Some, you know, people use the Pareto 2080 and say 80% of my data is really in unstructured formats of documents and drawings and audio and video.

229
00:29:41.350 --> 00:29:53.989
John Talburt: the move initially now has been to how do we structure what's unstructured? But I think beyond that, we're going to be thinking about how we do semantic computing.

230
00:29:54.100 --> 00:30:05.809
John Talburt: Where we… where we don't have to go through that transition phase of unstructured to structured, we simply introduce the unstructured data into the systems.

231
00:30:06.360 --> 00:30:12.860
John Talburt: Then the data quality, there's the Internet of Things, machine learning.

232
00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:21.460
John Talburt: And AI systems, especially now the new, language model systems that I just talked about,

233
00:30:21.590 --> 00:30:29.120
John Talburt: Automation of data quality assessment and cleansing. We've been working on a project here called the Data Washing Machine to

234
00:30:29.300 --> 00:30:31.160
John Talburt: Research on that.

235
00:30:31.370 --> 00:30:33.779
John Talburt: The automation of data governance.

236
00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:43.839
John Talburt: data literacy, data democratization, data monetization, and economics. So these are new topics that are coming out of the information quality world.

237
00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:48.169
John Talburt: So we'll be talking about some of these as we go through the course.

238
00:30:50.790 --> 00:30:57.109
John Talburt: So with that, I'd like to get started. If any questions, feel free to interrupt at any time, or any comments.

239
00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:11.220
John Talburt: So, we talk about this course as being Principles, so I've developed 7 principles. They might not all be comprehensive of everything, but it's a good start with it.

240
00:31:11.590 --> 00:31:17.660
John Talburt: So, principle number one, And I think most people now recognize this.

241
00:31:17.820 --> 00:31:20.819
John Talburt: That data is a resource which,

242
00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:33.350
John Talburt: you can produce information products for the purpose of creating value, so that we want to think about data and information from a value standpoint, like our definition.

243
00:31:33.900 --> 00:31:44.979
John Talburt: And data collected and processed and formatted to a particular use is what we call an information product. And I should add, and I probably will go back and revise some of these.

244
00:31:45.300 --> 00:31:51.460
John Talburt: It's not always just a product, a static product, it could be an information service as well.

245
00:31:53.140 --> 00:32:04.289
John Talburt: And, of course, whenever you take a resource, like data, and you create value from it, then that means that that thing becomes an asset, right?

246
00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:09.070
John Talburt: So now there's a recognition that information is an organizational asset.

247
00:32:09.940 --> 00:32:25.169
John Talburt: So data is the resource, we bring it, produce, use it to produce an information product or service, and by doing that, if we create value by doing that, now data and information become an asset.

248
00:32:27.860 --> 00:32:39.339
John Talburt: So, value, just to be sure that we all comprehend, I think we do, it's very simple in terms of a formula, although it's sometimes hard to figure out what goes into the formula.

249
00:32:40.110 --> 00:32:47.709
John Talburt: We create value whenever the benefits outweigh or larger than

250
00:32:48.110 --> 00:32:50.999
John Talburt: The investment that it takes to get that.

251
00:32:51.160 --> 00:32:52.210
John Talburt: benefit.

252
00:32:53.470 --> 00:32:57.730
John Talburt: So, you know, we always want to have positive value.

253
00:32:58.110 --> 00:33:04.500
John Talburt: We want that number to be positive. We want the benefits of what we're doing to outweigh the cost of doing them.

254
00:33:06.470 --> 00:33:15.560
John Talburt: And so, it's really, you know, creating value should be the reason that we do things. Whatever it is that we do in life, or…

255
00:33:15.880 --> 00:33:22.430
John Talburt: should be valued. The benefit of doing it should outweigh the cost of doing it, or otherwise, why would we do it?

256
00:33:22.920 --> 00:33:32.929
John Talburt: And as I mentioned earlier, We can measure value in 3 basic ways. Monetary, Quality of life.

257
00:33:33.600 --> 00:33:35.500
John Talburt: Or mission accomplishment.

258
00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:47.539
John Talburt: I think that covers pretty much all of the value proposition. So we don't want to just think in terms of money. There are other things that, create value as well, different types of value.

259
00:33:48.860 --> 00:33:58.370
John Talburt: And another problem we have, and this is one that I've suffered with a lot, especially when I was just mostly a technician, started off as a programmer.

260
00:33:58.760 --> 00:34:02.860
John Talburt: Is that we often confuse results with value.

261
00:34:03.870 --> 00:34:12.500
John Talburt: And what do I mean by that? So, we expend an effort to do something, right?

262
00:34:12.880 --> 00:34:14.550
John Talburt: And we,

263
00:34:14.760 --> 00:34:21.419
John Talburt: Go in and we say, well, we expended this effort, and here are the results, here's what we got out of it.

264
00:34:22.350 --> 00:34:26.649
John Talburt: And oftentimes we just stop there. For example.

265
00:34:26.889 --> 00:34:32.050
John Talburt: I ran into this when I was working at Axiom Corporation, and I would say, well.

266
00:34:32.409 --> 00:34:38.870
John Talburt: I was working in a project, and we were trying to integrate information

267
00:34:39.530 --> 00:34:49.150
John Talburt: And we measure that in terms of precision and recall, of how well, how accurately we integrated data together.

268
00:34:49.260 --> 00:34:53.510
John Talburt: And so I was working on a project, and the manager of the project, or the

269
00:34:53.520 --> 00:35:06.630
John Talburt: the project leader was making the presentation. He said, look, we spent, 3 months working on this, and we tried X, and we tried Y, and we tried Z, and we finally had success, and look.

270
00:35:06.630 --> 00:35:21.160
John Talburt: We have increased the precision of our integration process. We increased it from 88% to 92%, and the recall has increased from 70% to 80%.

271
00:35:21.620 --> 00:35:27.480
John Talburt: And he just stopped right there and looked at everyone as though they're going to really

272
00:35:28.340 --> 00:35:40.200
John Talburt: do wow, you know, and applaud, because that seemed to him, and to me, I was on the team, a huge success, right? Because those results, we improved the results dramatically.

273
00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:43.030
John Talburt: For that kind of an operation.

274
00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:45.089
John Talburt: May not seem like a lot, but…

275
00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:54.309
John Talburt: In data integration, master data management, moving precision and recall by just a few percentage points can be a real challenge.

276
00:35:54.920 --> 00:36:03.999
John Talburt: So, if you just stop there, then, you know, the business managers and the CEO, and the CMO, and the CEO,

277
00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:07.099
John Talburt: If they're standing in the room when you make that.

278
00:36:07.370 --> 00:36:23.130
John Talburt: you know, their eyes roll. I mean, so, okay, so what does that mean? And that's where we often drop the ball. We say, okay, we expend an effort, we make results, we get some kind of big improvement that we measure, but what is the value of that?

279
00:36:23.360 --> 00:36:35.840
John Talburt: Okay, so what difference does it make that I've moved the precision of our process from 88% to 92%? Does that mean we're going to retain more customers? Does that mean we lose less customers?

280
00:36:35.840 --> 00:36:49.349
John Talburt: Is it going to be cheaper to service our customers? Is it going to mean we can expand our market by 20%? Or, you know, give me some numbers that represent value, not technical.

281
00:36:49.420 --> 00:36:52.149
John Talburt: Statistics and percentages.

282
00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:57.289
John Talburt: So, the way to tell the difference between results and value, typically, is that

283
00:36:57.780 --> 00:37:04.460
John Talburt: We talk about them in different ways, right? Results, we talk about as statistics, as percentages.

284
00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:11.969
John Talburt: But value is, we talk about in terms of increasing revenue, or lowering cost, or lowering mortality, and…

285
00:37:12.120 --> 00:37:17.890
John Talburt: In medical, or higher success rates, and mission accomplishment, or whatever.

286
00:37:18.130 --> 00:37:23.510
John Talburt: What… how do we measure it in terms of what we're trying to… the value that we're trying to create?

287
00:37:23.980 --> 00:37:28.039
John Talburt: So, this was a valuable lesson to me, and…

288
00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:38.530
John Talburt: I learned that this… to complete this value chain, you have to go from results to value. You can't just say, here's better results.

289
00:37:38.750 --> 00:37:44.040
John Talburt: You need to think about, okay, do the better results then give me more value?

290
00:37:44.600 --> 00:37:53.100
John Talburt: we oftentimes just leave that unstated, and we assume that people understand. So, going back to my example about the

291
00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:56.329
John Talburt: The integration system that we were improving.

292
00:37:56.550 --> 00:38:02.330
John Talburt: All of the members of the team were just overjoyed that we went from 88% to 92%.

293
00:38:02.570 --> 00:38:05.670
John Talburt: Precision. We knew that that was a huge lift.

294
00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:13.520
John Talburt: And we were rejoicing about it, but anyone from the outside that didn't understand all the technical details, it was meaningless to them.

295
00:38:13.700 --> 00:38:24.470
John Talburt: Because we assumed they understood, yeah, now this system's better, it's gonna mean better for the business, but we never said, how. How is it gonna be better for the business?

296
00:38:25.320 --> 00:38:32.830
John Talburt: So… Completing the value chain, don't confuse just stating results with stating what is the value.

297
00:38:35.660 --> 00:38:41.780
John Talburt: Simple example here is that we have 5… and because some of these projects are really big.

298
00:38:42.090 --> 00:38:49.149
John Talburt: A 5-man-year system development with a million dollars of direct costs and $500,000 of indirect costs.

299
00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:55.770
John Talburt: The result? A new customer recognition system, 15% more accurate than the legacy system.

300
00:38:56.030 --> 00:38:56.880
John Talburt: Wow.

301
00:38:57.150 --> 00:39:07.760
John Talburt: 15%. If we just leave it there, and we say, well, people understand, well, it's 15% better, better is better, right? But look, we spent $1.5 million doing this.

302
00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:18.240
John Talburt: So, to get to better results that we can interpret, survey shows, well, after doing this, the customer satisfaction is up 20%,

303
00:39:18.440 --> 00:39:25.530
John Talburt: Another result… Keeping going with the chain. Analysis shows customer retention is up by 8%.

304
00:39:26.010 --> 00:39:34.639
John Talburt: Now we can get to a value. The difference between customer acquisition costs and customer maintenance costs projects.

305
00:39:34.890 --> 00:39:43.719
John Talburt: projects a net revenue increase over 5 years of $20 million, which more than makes up for the cost of it.

306
00:39:43.900 --> 00:39:48.100
John Talburt: Right? We know in our business, most businesses know.

307
00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:56.380
John Talburt: that it costs less to maintain a customer than we had… than to go out and market and get a new customer. And if you take that number.

308
00:39:56.460 --> 00:40:09.880
John Talburt: and project it by the smaller, or the greater retention, the smaller loss rate of customers, you can turn that into a value, a number, in this case, $20 million.

309
00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:16.699
John Talburt: So, we need to keep going with the value chain until we get from results to a value.

310
00:40:19.720 --> 00:40:34.910
John Talburt: So, again, I spent a lot of time, but that's the very important principle of information quality, is it needs to be based on value. We don't want to think about information quality as just cleaning up dirty data.

311
00:40:35.090 --> 00:40:47.489
John Talburt: Nobody's excited about that. It's just like thinking we build a new building, yeah, we gotta have janitors. We know the floor's gonna get dirty. We gotta keep cleaning them, but, you know, what good is that?

312
00:40:48.070 --> 00:40:53.390
John Talburt: Other than just having clean floors. So, we want to have a bigger picture, bigger thought.

313
00:40:53.560 --> 00:40:58.680
John Talburt: And it's not… it's understanding what customers need, what kind of

314
00:40:58.810 --> 00:41:16.159
John Talburt: information, products, and services do they need and want and that they will pay for? How do we make it a quality product or service? And then we can work backwards and see what data, what condition does the data need to be in to build that quality product or service.

315
00:41:17.640 --> 00:41:22.740
John Talburt: So, value's the first principle. The second principle is,

316
00:41:23.310 --> 00:41:29.220
John Talburt: As we already mentioned in the history, is the recognition that, okay, if we're making money.

317
00:41:29.490 --> 00:41:37.159
John Talburt: Or, you know, improving the quality of life or accomplishing our mission with better data and information.

318
00:41:37.650 --> 00:41:41.419
John Talburt: then it's an organizational asset. We need to control it.

319
00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:47.779
John Talburt: And controlling data as an asset is called data governance.

320
00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:53.290
John Talburt: The way I like to think about data governance is, this.

321
00:41:54.500 --> 00:41:57.549
John Talburt: Data governance is not data management.

322
00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:04.829
John Talburt: What we do with data is data management. We decide what we're going to do with data.

323
00:42:05.010 --> 00:42:11.410
John Talburt: data governance, It's the set of rules for making the decisions about what we do with it.

324
00:42:12.050 --> 00:42:19.679
John Talburt: So it's sort of a meta level. If data management are the decisions about what to do with data.

325
00:42:19.850 --> 00:42:23.469
John Talburt: Data governance is the guidelines for making those decisions.

326
00:42:24.160 --> 00:42:29.649
John Talburt: So, it's very important, right? I mean, if something's, important to you, I mean.

327
00:42:30.460 --> 00:42:35.900
John Talburt: There's a… there's a famous story about a guy going in to make a

328
00:42:36.700 --> 00:42:41.500
John Talburt: A pitch about data governance and, The executive just…

329
00:42:41.680 --> 00:42:49.050
John Talburt: kind of, you know, poo-hooing everything that he said, and just said, you know, what you're doing is not important. We don't need to…

330
00:42:49.370 --> 00:42:51.090
John Talburt: To control all that data.

331
00:42:51.470 --> 00:42:56.560
John Talburt: And, as he was leaving the meeting, he just stopped in the restroom, and he noticed that

332
00:42:56.710 --> 00:42:59.559
John Talburt: All the urinals had inventory numbers on them.

333
00:42:59.690 --> 00:43:07.950
John Talburt: So it was a company that was wanting to really take control of their assets by numbering all the urinals in the bathroom, but they didn't want to,

334
00:43:08.420 --> 00:43:13.869
John Talburt: To engage in data governance, to take care of the data that they were using day-to-day.

335
00:43:14.310 --> 00:43:19.480
John Talburt: So, however, it's now one of the fastest growing areas of information quality.

336
00:43:21.170 --> 00:43:26.119
John Talburt: Because, Everyone is interested in how they govern data.

337
00:43:26.360 --> 00:43:35.239
John Talburt: Not only to create more value out of it, but to… remember in our definition, it's not only to maximize value, but to minimize risk.

338
00:43:35.820 --> 00:43:42.130
John Talburt: And so there's huge risk with information. You can talk to Meta about that today, I guess.

339
00:43:43.820 --> 00:43:52.670
John Talburt: But there's a lot of regulation, a lot of oversight, especially in the financial services area and other regulated areas.

340
00:43:53.020 --> 00:44:01.029
John Talburt: That if you're not careful with how you use data, what you use it for, how you release data, how you protect data.

341
00:44:01.220 --> 00:44:04.330
John Talburt: You can have huge liabilities.

342
00:44:04.580 --> 00:44:12.590
John Talburt: So, data governance is both sides. It's… it's building… it's helping to have a plan for

343
00:44:12.920 --> 00:44:17.019
John Talburt: Building the right things, but also doing the right things with your data.

344
00:44:19.040 --> 00:44:25.350
John Talburt: So, in the same way that every business has an accounting function, and

345
00:44:25.480 --> 00:44:28.900
John Talburt: their technology, we typically have a CIO.

346
00:44:29.180 --> 00:44:35.350
John Talburt: there… then this has really taken off, since I did these slides,

347
00:44:36.160 --> 00:44:43.649
John Talburt: Of course, the federal government now has passed the regulation that every federal agency should have a chief data officer.

348
00:44:44.270 --> 00:44:45.480
John Talburt: And,

349
00:44:49.050 --> 00:44:52.979
John Talburt: So, if we're really going to manage information as an asset.

350
00:44:53.150 --> 00:45:00.679
John Talburt: Somebody needs to be the gatekeeper or caretaker, and that generally falls as the role of the chief data officer.

351
00:45:01.490 --> 00:45:04.050
John Talburt: They should be in charge of the strategy.

352
00:45:04.360 --> 00:45:10.629
John Talburt: For how… what… what information products and services are we gonna build for our customers?

353
00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:16.080
John Talburt: How do we get that information? How do we manage it? How do we govern it?

354
00:45:16.410 --> 00:45:20.220
John Talburt: Somebody needs to be in charge of that.

355
00:45:20.970 --> 00:45:25.240
John Talburt: So, we talked about value, we talked about, governance.

356
00:45:25.710 --> 00:45:31.290
John Talburt: Then we need to talk about some of the fundamentals, and this one, I got in a little bit of a…

357
00:45:31.610 --> 00:45:35.379
John Talburt: argument with Doug Laney about this, but I think I'm okay.

358
00:45:35.620 --> 00:45:42.449
John Talburt: Information can only produce value when it's used for some purpose.

359
00:45:43.220 --> 00:45:44.780
John Talburt: And,

360
00:45:46.730 --> 00:45:56.539
John Talburt: The argument that we had over it is he made the statement that he disliked it when people say that information only has value when it's used.

361
00:45:57.330 --> 00:46:02.090
John Talburt: And I agree with him, that's not true. It's not,

362
00:46:03.170 --> 00:46:22.239
John Talburt: has value, but does it produce value? So it only produces the value when it's used. If you have data and you're not using it, it has potential value, just like something… a weight on a shelf has a potential energy when it falls off and expends that energy.

363
00:46:22.270 --> 00:46:34.479
John Talburt: The same thing is true with data. So, it was sort of a misunderstanding in terminology. I never said that information only has value when it's used. It only produces value when it's used.

364
00:46:34.570 --> 00:46:36.819
John Talburt: Certainly, it has potential value.

365
00:46:37.700 --> 00:46:47.520
John Talburt: So, transforming, data into a form for use, again, is back to this product idea.

366
00:46:49.140 --> 00:46:59.930
John Talburt: And the quality of an information product is measured in terms of the value, right? So again, we're seeing this… we're going to see this divide, and I'll make it a little clearer later.

367
00:47:00.360 --> 00:47:04.709
John Talburt: That I make, anyway, and of course, I'm the teacher, so I can do this.

368
00:47:04.950 --> 00:47:09.270
John Talburt: Between data quality and information quality.

369
00:47:09.880 --> 00:47:20.859
John Talburt: So, information quality, of our definition, is based on value. It's based on producing value, maximizing value, or minimizing risk.

370
00:47:21.110 --> 00:47:23.760
John Talburt: Of our information assets.

371
00:47:27.610 --> 00:47:29.630
John Talburt: So, principle number four.

372
00:47:30.210 --> 00:47:39.410
John Talburt: And then, as we just mentioned in the previous slide, if we're building this product, then really information should be managed as product.

373
00:47:40.150 --> 00:47:44.990
John Talburt: The product of an information system Not a byproduct.

374
00:47:45.220 --> 00:47:52.079
John Talburt: The reason that we invest in all these computers, the reason we invest in all these cloud services and servers.

375
00:47:52.340 --> 00:47:54.200
John Talburt: is not,

376
00:47:55.470 --> 00:48:06.620
John Talburt: is to produce some kind of a product or information product or service. It's not… just happens to be something that comes out of the system. That's why we got them in the first place.

377
00:48:07.520 --> 00:48:14.839
John Talburt: So, managing information as a byproduct of computing is just not… doesn't make sense.

378
00:48:14.960 --> 00:48:23.960
John Talburt: But often you see that, and that's the reason that we've seen the shift. You know, most organizations for many years have had a chief information officer.

379
00:48:24.180 --> 00:48:37.309
John Talburt: So the question was, well, then why do we need a chief data officer if we're already managing? But the problem is, even though chief information officers have that title, that's not what they do.

380
00:48:37.380 --> 00:48:56.900
John Talburt: CIOs focus on systems, software, and hardware. They're wanting to be sure that the system uptime is maximized, that they have the latest versions of the software, and it's all a technical focus, and it's not focused on the data. And that's the reason that we've needed a chief data officer.

381
00:48:56.990 --> 00:49:03.479
John Talburt: Even though we already have a Chief Information Officer, there… even though the names…

382
00:49:03.650 --> 00:49:07.520
John Talburt: The names are not aligned with what they actually do.

383
00:49:07.980 --> 00:49:15.729
John Talburt: And, so… and I've seen this so many times in organizations that

384
00:49:15.970 --> 00:49:27.070
John Talburt: The means becomes the end. And what's really tragic is that many have kind of caught on to this idea, well, sure, okay, now there's a trend, we need to get a chief data officer.

385
00:49:27.230 --> 00:49:34.029
John Talburt: And what they do is they create a chief data officer position, but they put it under the CIO.

386
00:49:34.210 --> 00:49:38.919
John Talburt: And that usually means that it's not going to be very successful.

387
00:49:39.150 --> 00:49:51.170
John Talburt: The chief data officer and the chief information officer should be peers. They should be at the same level reporting up to higher levels, like the chief operations officer, or the

388
00:49:51.370 --> 00:49:54.370
John Talburt: marketing officer, or even the CEO.

389
00:49:54.890 --> 00:50:03.800
John Talburt: Because, really, if you don't have a balance between your data and your technology.

390
00:50:03.920 --> 00:50:14.519
John Talburt: You're, and you're only… and you have a higher priority for the technology, you're never gonna, get the full value out of the data that you have.

391
00:50:16.090 --> 00:50:22.169
John Talburt: So, how do you successfully manage project… products, I'm sorry, successful product management?

392
00:50:22.370 --> 00:50:26.679
John Talburt: And we've known that for a long time, and again, most of this comes from TQM.

393
00:50:27.190 --> 00:50:29.270
John Talburt: There's 4 main principles.

394
00:50:29.920 --> 00:50:34.590
John Talburt: Number one, understand the information customer needs.

395
00:50:35.130 --> 00:50:37.930
John Talburt: What information do your customers really need?

396
00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:47.780
John Talburt: And what do they want? Whether it's information or anything, a car or whatever, what is it that the customer's wanting? You need to understand that.

397
00:50:49.000 --> 00:50:54.479
John Talburt: And then manage information is the product of a well-defined production process.

398
00:50:54.960 --> 00:51:00.330
John Talburt: And again, unfortunately, When we build products.

399
00:51:00.500 --> 00:51:07.910
John Talburt: Of course, as soon as it's built, we have this beautiful set of documentation, and everybody understands what's going on.

400
00:51:08.090 --> 00:51:14.150
John Talburt: But as time goes on, we start patching and fixing, and it goes undocumented.

401
00:51:14.270 --> 00:51:17.300
John Talburt: And oftentimes, in several years.

402
00:51:17.450 --> 00:51:22.699
John Talburt: You know, the only… there's one person in the company that knows how a particular process works.

403
00:51:22.800 --> 00:51:26.220
John Talburt: And if he retires, then you have to abandon the system.

404
00:51:26.480 --> 00:51:31.540
John Talburt: So, we really need well-defined, well-documented production processes.

405
00:51:32.300 --> 00:51:37.129
John Talburt: And then the other is, I mentioned earlier about, really liking the,

406
00:51:37.690 --> 00:51:42.569
John Talburt: El Camino, but you can't really buy them anymore, except on the aftermarket.

407
00:51:42.770 --> 00:51:52.550
John Talburt: And the reason is that, you know, there was… there's been shifts in the automotive market. We've gone through a huge cycle now of,

408
00:51:52.740 --> 00:52:11.570
John Talburt: SUVs. We used to have station wagons. No, not anymore. We have SUVs, we have pickup trucks. So, all products have a life cycle. You initiate the product, it grows, it reaches maturity, it's useful, but at some point, it retires, right?

409
00:52:11.790 --> 00:52:21.890
John Talburt: It's like a plant or an animal has a life cycle. Products have a life cycle. So understanding where you are in the life cycle is very important in managing a product.

410
00:52:23.490 --> 00:52:35.159
John Talburt: And then this is one that we really need, in the information quality area, and I see this happening more and more in businesses, is the appointment of product managers.

411
00:52:35.680 --> 00:52:41.790
John Talburt: And the purpose of a product manager, and manufacturing has understood this for a long time.

412
00:52:41.970 --> 00:52:49.979
John Talburt: Is you need someone that's the liaison between the consumer of the product and the producer of the product.

413
00:52:50.630 --> 00:53:01.370
John Talburt: If you just leave it to the producers to imagine what they want to build, they often end up building things that are just not useful or not sellable to their consumers.

414
00:53:01.680 --> 00:53:05.810
John Talburt: So, it's very important to listen to the consumer, what they want.

415
00:53:05.970 --> 00:53:10.750
John Talburt: Translate that back into production so that you meet consumer needs.

416
00:53:11.210 --> 00:53:17.299
John Talburt: And, we've done that a lot in software development. Software development has had

417
00:53:17.410 --> 00:53:20.380
John Talburt: Product managers for a long time.

418
00:53:20.580 --> 00:53:31.260
John Talburt: So people that sell software, they go out and they talk, what features do you want, what other things do you want in the software? They take it back, software developers implement those things.

419
00:53:31.380 --> 00:53:39.249
John Talburt: Not so much until lately for information, products, and services, but I do see that happening. I worked with a company

420
00:53:39.390 --> 00:53:50.290
John Talburt: for several years, doing some consulting, and I think it was 2 years ago, they implemented an information product manager group.

421
00:53:50.690 --> 00:53:54.779
John Talburt: So they identified what are the information products that we're producing.

422
00:53:55.050 --> 00:54:03.330
John Talburt: And who's the assigned manager for that? Who's the liaison between the user of this product and the producers of the product?

423
00:54:03.800 --> 00:54:07.580
John Talburt: So those are the four principles of successful product management.

424
00:54:08.730 --> 00:54:10.080
John Talburt: Number 5…

425
00:54:12.570 --> 00:54:18.349
John Talburt: And this is what I talked about earlier, and now we talked a little bit about the difference between information quality and data quality.

426
00:54:18.510 --> 00:54:22.539
John Talburt: And they're certainly related, but they're different in their focus.

427
00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:25.330
John Talburt: Information quality.

428
00:54:26.050 --> 00:54:30.659
John Talburt: Applies to information products, including their governance and use.

429
00:54:31.060 --> 00:54:40.450
John Talburt: It's measured in value units, like money, or quality of life, you know, success rates of drugs, or mission achievement.

430
00:54:41.080 --> 00:54:51.370
John Talburt: And that's… the information quality is the overall process of going from raw information, or raw data, Processing

431
00:54:51.720 --> 00:55:00.670
John Talburt: Producing information products, the loopback of improvement, the governance of it, all of this infrastructure, our ecosystem.

432
00:55:00.800 --> 00:55:02.969
John Talburt: To me, is information quality.

433
00:55:03.490 --> 00:55:10.660
John Talburt: Data quality is a big part of that, but it applies to operational data internal to the organization.

434
00:55:11.300 --> 00:55:16.349
John Talburt: And it's measured in percentages, the degrees to which data requirements are met.

435
00:55:16.740 --> 00:55:21.960
John Talburt: So, those of you who are very familiar, some of you, with the idea of,

436
00:55:22.430 --> 00:55:27.090
John Talburt: of ISO, the International Organization of Standardization.

437
00:55:27.770 --> 00:55:34.919
John Talburt: That, re… that quality The definition of quality is meeting requirements.

438
00:55:35.360 --> 00:55:39.349
John Talburt: So, data quality is meeting data requirements, so…

439
00:55:39.780 --> 00:55:44.169
John Talburt: We have a requirement, for example, completeness of the data.

440
00:55:44.760 --> 00:55:50.639
John Talburt: We have a data set of customer addresses, and we say, well.

441
00:55:50.860 --> 00:56:09.040
John Talburt: you know, we're missing a lot of zip codes for our addresses. We need those zip codes to get the mail to the right place. So let's measure how complete are the zip codes. And they say, well, they're only 80% complete. And we can set a goal, and we say, well, okay, we want to get those to 90%, right?

442
00:56:09.170 --> 00:56:13.919
John Talburt: So we measure, we have a metric, a completeness metric.

443
00:56:14.170 --> 00:56:17.950
John Talburt: And we can measure that, we can count how many,

444
00:56:18.480 --> 00:56:22.739
John Talburt: How many missing values that we have, and we can strive to reduce that.

445
00:56:22.750 --> 00:56:40.350
John Talburt: And so that's improving the data quality. So data quality is measured in terms of some metric around… that we usually have a numerical value for that's a degree to which we're meeting the requirement. How far are we from the 90% goal that we want to get to?

446
00:56:41.000 --> 00:56:48.999
John Talburt: So that's data quality. Information quality is the much bigger picture of value production from information products and services.

447
00:56:50.910 --> 00:57:04.389
John Talburt: So, again, information is a lot of squabbling about this sometimes, and a lot of disagreement. People say, well, these terms are just interchangeable, and we do sometimes. Even I will not necessarily make a big distinction.

448
00:57:05.990 --> 00:57:10.839
John Talburt: But I like to think, again, of this two sides of the coin, where,

449
00:57:11.380 --> 00:57:14.320
John Talburt: The data quality has an inward focus.

450
00:57:14.840 --> 00:57:26.410
John Talburt: Because it's dealing with the condition of the data. It's measured in conformance to data requirements, right? Data quality is the degree to which data meets data requirements.

451
00:57:27.270 --> 00:57:32.660
John Talburt: And we measured in counts, percentages, proportions, ratios, things like that.

452
00:57:33.310 --> 00:57:39.349
John Talburt: Information quality has an outward focus. It's about creating value from information.

453
00:57:39.780 --> 00:57:46.180
John Talburt: About meeting the user requirements and expectations, which are not always measured as completeness or…

454
00:57:46.280 --> 00:57:52.959
John Talburt: In other words, they have a… they have other types of evaluations of how useful something is to them.

455
00:57:53.720 --> 00:58:06.620
John Talburt: And information quality measures are, you know, in these fields of value. If it's monetary, it could be monetary, could be treatment outcomes, if we're talking about medical, and so forth.

456
00:58:07.100 --> 00:58:10.300
John Talburt: So… The focus is different.

457
00:58:10.490 --> 00:58:17.909
John Talburt: And it's like the engineering dilemma. Are you building the thing right, or are you building the right thing?

458
00:58:19.560 --> 00:58:24.349
John Talburt: And this is very, similar to Tom Redmond's,

459
00:58:24.720 --> 00:58:28.130
John Talburt: Saul that's similar to this. He says,

460
00:58:28.280 --> 00:58:32.209
John Talburt: Is the data right, or do you have the right data?

461
00:58:32.700 --> 00:58:36.270
John Talburt: And you really want both, right? So this is saying the same thing.

462
00:58:36.990 --> 00:58:42.199
John Talburt: So, data quality is building the thing right.

463
00:58:42.980 --> 00:58:59.779
John Talburt: In other words, are you cleaning up the data? You're getting the data, like, in the data warehouse? You're getting it to be consistent representation? All of these attributes of the data, you try to get those right. But again, no matter how right those…

464
00:58:59.930 --> 00:59:09.790
John Talburt: the data are, when you put it together into a product, if it's a product that nobody wants, or doesn't sell well, or is defective.

465
00:59:10.360 --> 00:59:18.199
John Talburt: you're not building the right thing. So you really need to start off and say, am I building the right thing? I'm gonna build a building on this lot.

466
00:59:18.830 --> 00:59:25.270
John Talburt: But if I build a building on this lot, and it's the type of building that I build, let's say it's an apartment building.

467
00:59:25.470 --> 00:59:34.460
John Talburt: Is that… is that the right thing to do? Is it really going to sell or rent if I build this apartment in this particular location?

468
00:59:34.620 --> 00:59:48.350
John Talburt: If the answer is yes, then I want to be sure that when I build that apartment, I build it correctly, I build it according to the blueprints and the plans. If I just go out and say, oh, here's a piece of land, I'm going to build this building because I like it.

469
00:59:48.590 --> 01:00:00.730
John Talburt: and I build it, and I can build that building right, I can build it according to the blueprint, but if I build it in the wrong place, or it's the wrong type of building for that location, it's not the right thing.

470
01:00:00.910 --> 01:00:09.190
John Talburt: And so you need to align both of these things. So, as Tom said, you need the right data, and you need the data to be right.

471
01:00:11.510 --> 01:00:16.769
John Talburt: So, data quality is assuring data conforms to specifications, like building it right.

472
01:00:17.340 --> 01:00:24.559
John Talburt: And information quality is building an information product that creates value for the user, and that's building the right thing.

473
01:00:26.080 --> 01:00:27.869
John Talburt: Our goal is to do both.

474
01:00:30.500 --> 01:00:36.360
John Talburt: So, that leads us to what I call the information quality gap.

475
01:00:37.300 --> 01:00:46.480
John Talburt: And what I mean by that is that we have a process where we're building an information product. We've got data coming in, we have a process.

476
01:00:46.670 --> 01:00:58.319
John Talburt: And we're doing the right things. We've got a continuous improvement, we keep improving the information product, and we meet the data requirements internally.

477
01:00:58.660 --> 01:01:03.399
John Talburt: However, That's the internal view, that's data quality management.

478
01:01:03.700 --> 01:01:16.000
John Talburt: But we're delivering this information product to some user. It could be within our own organization or outside of our organization, but there's somebody using this, information, product, or service.

479
01:01:17.020 --> 01:01:21.729
John Talburt: And they're… they're… they have a… they're using it for something.

480
01:01:22.090 --> 01:01:25.040
John Talburt: They have an application that they're using it for.

481
01:01:25.380 --> 01:01:26.380
John Talburt: So…

482
01:01:26.630 --> 01:01:33.680
John Talburt: That information product, that's what creates the value for the user, is it doing the right thing for them.

483
01:01:34.180 --> 01:01:40.540
John Talburt: And so, if we don't communicate this back, and this is where we need the idea of a product manager.

484
01:01:40.990 --> 01:01:50.959
John Talburt: Then, these happen in isolation, and we can end up over here on the left-hand side building things that are pretty, gold-plated.

485
01:01:51.140 --> 01:01:56.869
John Talburt: But they're not the things that people ask for that they really want when they use it.

486
01:01:57.360 --> 01:02:09.249
John Talburt: So, we need that communication about what's really creating value for the user, so that we can be sure we incorporate that into the building process of the information, product, or service.

487
01:02:10.150 --> 01:02:26.149
John Talburt: So what we want is this, we don't want that gap. We want another feedback loop. Not just a feedback loop of defects in the product, but a feedback loop that says, what are the features that are going to make that information product more useful to the user?

488
01:02:29.930 --> 01:02:35.910
John Talburt: Principle number 6, and this is… helps make the distinction.

489
01:02:36.410 --> 01:02:43.770
John Talburt: Is that, information quality, I'm sorry, I just…

490
01:02:44.220 --> 01:02:45.920
John Talburt: Oh, how that got out of order.

491
01:02:46.250 --> 01:02:57.980
John Talburt: Information quality is contextual, that is, situational, it's not absolute. The same data can have different information quality when used in different information products for different purposes.

492
01:02:58.480 --> 01:03:08.290
John Talburt: The same data with the same set of data requirements has a fixed data quality. In other words, you set requirements, it's either meeting those requirements or it's not.

493
01:03:08.970 --> 01:03:13.929
John Talburt: So the data quality changes when either the data or the data requirements change.

494
01:03:14.730 --> 01:03:20.170
John Talburt: However, changes in the use of information products can change the data requirements.

495
01:03:20.400 --> 01:03:23.790
John Talburt: That's what I mean by that. Let's look at a very simple example.

496
01:03:24.690 --> 01:03:29.549
John Talburt: I've got a file of name and address, right? So here's some examples.

497
01:03:29.720 --> 01:03:32.789
John Talburt: I've got the doe family on 123 Oak.

498
01:03:33.210 --> 01:03:35.189
John Talburt: With a certain phone number.

499
01:03:36.050 --> 01:03:42.870
John Talburt: the John Doe family on Pine Street. I don't have a number. I've got Occupant on 789 Maple.

500
01:03:43.280 --> 01:03:44.900
John Talburt: I don't have a phone number.

501
01:03:45.790 --> 01:03:52.369
John Talburt: And so forth and so on. So, what the bottom line is that I have poor name quality.

502
01:03:52.970 --> 01:03:54.980
John Talburt: Have good address quality.

503
01:03:55.540 --> 01:04:01.349
John Talburt: But I have also a lot of missing telephone numbers. So from a data quality standpoint.

504
01:04:01.870 --> 01:04:12.199
John Talburt: I can measure these. I can measure how good my names are, how good my addresses are, how good my telephone numbers are, and I can see that I've got

505
01:04:12.660 --> 01:04:23.860
John Talburt: fairly low, poor name quality, and I've got poor telephone number quality, because I have a lot of missing numbers, but I have pretty good address quality. So now, how am I going to use this data?

506
01:04:23.870 --> 01:04:37.650
John Talburt: Suppose the first use is I'm going to mail coupons out for free pizza. There's a new pizza store opening, and they want to give the customer, you bring in the coupon, you'll get one free pizza.

507
01:04:38.170 --> 01:04:51.019
John Talburt: And it turns out that the use of this data for that is very high. It's a high-quality product. Even though the data has a lot of low-college features, the main feature that it needs is it needs to be mailable.

508
01:04:51.170 --> 01:04:55.879
John Talburt: Doesn't matter if it mails it to occupants, somebody's gonna get it at that house.

509
01:04:56.220 --> 01:05:03.529
John Talburt: And they're gonna get that coupon, and then they'll go down to Joe's Pizza, and he's got his customer prospect coming in.

510
01:05:03.940 --> 01:05:15.149
John Talburt: So, the fact that it has poor name quality, and they're not calling them, they're mailing them. So, it actually created this idea of a direct mail list out of this.

511
01:05:15.320 --> 01:05:35.100
John Talburt: is a high-quality information product, has high value. It's reaching the customers, or potential customers. However, if we take that same data, and we're going to make an online lookup of address and phone by name, in other words, you put in the name and you're going to try to find an address and a phone number.

512
01:05:35.190 --> 01:05:43.400
John Talburt: It's not going to work very well, because the name quality is very poor. So there'll be a lot of names I won't be able to look up because they're not there.

513
01:05:43.570 --> 01:05:53.640
John Talburt: And even if I looked them up, I could get an address, but I don't want to always get a phone, because there's a lot of missing phone days. So building that product out of this data.

514
01:05:53.810 --> 01:05:55.690
John Talburt: Is very low quality.

515
01:05:55.980 --> 01:05:58.800
John Talburt: It's a very low-quality, product.

516
01:05:59.010 --> 01:06:01.930
John Talburt: The, name lookup.

517
01:06:02.220 --> 01:06:13.359
John Talburt: So, here's an example where we have the same set of data, and it creates a high-quality product for one use, and a low-quality product for another use.

518
01:06:14.750 --> 01:06:18.800
John Talburt: Same data quality, but different information quality.

519
01:06:21.670 --> 01:06:26.090
John Talburt: The final principle is that, and we've been talking about this.

520
01:06:26.250 --> 01:06:36.430
John Talburt: is that data can have many characteristics that we like to call dimensions that can be used to express data requirements. So this is important for data quality.

521
01:06:37.040 --> 01:06:41.979
John Talburt: And the one that we like to use, and here's an unfortunate thing.

522
01:06:42.600 --> 01:06:54.410
John Talburt: And it goes back to a statement that I made earlier, is that the beginnings of information quality started with consultants, people like Larry English and Tom Redman and others.

523
01:06:54.560 --> 01:06:59.890
John Talburt: That… that went out, and of course, when you're a consultant, you want to distinguish yourself.

524
01:07:00.250 --> 01:07:04.629
John Talburt: And so you don't want to look like somebody else. You definitely want to be different.

525
01:07:04.950 --> 01:07:13.470
John Talburt: So what happened is, in the beginning, we had many, many different types or descriptions of data quality dimensions.

526
01:07:13.990 --> 01:07:21.500
John Talburt: So there are many of them floating out there. They're all quite similar, but they're all quite different in some ways.

527
01:07:22.080 --> 01:07:26.970
John Talburt: And the reason I like this one, this Wong Strong framework came out of,

528
01:07:27.220 --> 01:07:31.840
John Talburt: university work at MIT, and it has research backing behind it.

529
01:07:32.330 --> 01:07:37.810
John Talburt: So they came up with 16 dimensions, or features, of data.

530
01:07:38.360 --> 01:07:47.310
John Talburt: And they categorized them into four categories. Intrinsic dimensions, contextual dimensions, representational dimensions, and accessibility dimensions.

531
01:07:48.190 --> 01:07:55.819
John Talburt: And you can see them here. Intrinsic is… is… are the data accurate, believable, objective, reputation?

532
01:07:56.600 --> 01:08:08.130
John Talburt: Contextual, does it add value? Is it relevant? Timely? Complete? Is it the right amount of data? Representational? Is it interpretable? Understandable?

533
01:08:08.250 --> 01:08:13.399
John Talburt: Is it consistent? That's what we talked about earlier, representational consistency.

534
01:08:13.900 --> 01:08:17.170
John Talburt: Concise in representation isn't manipulable.

535
01:08:17.740 --> 01:08:21.789
John Talburt: And then accessibility, can I get to it, and is it secure?

536
01:08:22.149 --> 01:08:24.710
John Talburt: And those two kind of fight against each other.

537
01:08:25.390 --> 01:08:30.280
John Talburt: so these,

538
01:08:30.500 --> 01:08:39.340
John Talburt: Again, there are many other sets of dimensions. Most practitioners have their own set of dimensions, but they tend to agree on certain things, like…

539
01:08:39.680 --> 01:08:44.469
John Talburt: Accuracy, or timeliness, or completeness, or representation.

540
01:08:44.950 --> 01:08:54.600
John Talburt: On others, they might disagree on things like manipulability, or representation, or, several other types of…

541
01:08:56.069 --> 01:08:59.310
John Talburt: Features that other people come up with.

542
01:08:59.750 --> 01:09:10.379
John Talburt: But this is the one that we typically work with, again, because it's… there's a… there's a very interesting paper. I don't know if I put it out in the resources yet, but there's a good paper about how this was developed.

543
01:09:11.210 --> 01:09:18.349
John Talburt: And so we'll mostly be using this set of dimensions when we talk about dimensions.

544
01:09:19.760 --> 01:09:24.530
John Talburt: And of course, they're very important, then, for being able to measure data quality.

545
01:09:25.350 --> 01:09:28.260
John Talburt: So I mentioned this earlier, and I'll mention it again.

546
01:09:28.410 --> 01:09:37.100
John Talburt: the work that the International Association for Information and Data Quality did a job analysis in preparation for a certification

547
01:09:37.370 --> 01:09:42.659
John Talburt: A certified, Data quality, professional.

548
01:09:44.580 --> 01:09:47.800
John Talburt: I'm sorry, Certified Information Quality Professional.

549
01:09:48.200 --> 01:09:52.150
John Talburt: And… Again, the six domains are…

550
01:09:52.640 --> 01:10:01.720
John Talburt: Information quality value and business impact. Information Quality Strategy and Governance, Information Quality Environment and Culture.

551
01:10:01.920 --> 01:10:05.170
John Talburt: Information Quality Measurement Improvement.

552
01:10:05.430 --> 01:10:10.080
John Talburt: And sustaining information quality, and information architecture quality.

553
01:10:10.460 --> 01:10:17.269
John Talburt: So, they divided up the knowledge and practice that you need to be a professional into these 6 areas.

554
01:10:18.260 --> 01:10:20.430
John Talburt: We'll talk more about those later.

555
01:10:21.690 --> 01:10:26.280
John Talburt: So, what are some of the roles that our graduates get?

556
01:10:29.250 --> 01:10:36.310
John Talburt: They often become data quality analysts, data governance stewards, or directors.

557
01:10:36.420 --> 01:10:44.030
John Talburt: Solution developers, depending on their background, data developers, and we've had several now that have become chief data officers.

558
01:10:45.630 --> 01:10:51.260
John Talburt: And many that are now data analysts and data… risk data analysts as well.

559
01:10:51.830 --> 01:10:55.869
John Talburt: And a lot of… a lot of our graduates are now into data governance.

560
01:10:57.350 --> 01:11:05.530
John Talburt: So, I think we're running up against time. I don't know if there's any quick questions or comments from anyone still online.

561
01:11:08.000 --> 01:11:14.389
John Talburt: If not, now I'm going to stop the recording.

